10. FineCalculator — Strategy pattern
interface FineCalculator {
    double calculate(BookLoan loan, LocalDate date);
}


The interface defines what every fine calculator must implement.

The concrete implementation is:
class DailyFineCalculator implements FineCalculator {
    private final double dailyRate;

    @Override
    public double calculate(BookLoan loan, LocalDate date) {
        long days = ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(
                loan.getDueDate(), date);

        return Math.max(0, days) * dailyRate;
    }
}


Suppose:
- Due date: October 11
- Return date: October 15
- Daily fine: ₹5
The calculation is:
\[
\text{Overdue days}=15-11=4
\]

\[
\text{Fine}=4\times 5=\text{₹}20
\]

If the book is returned on or before the due date, the fine is zero.

Math.max(0, days) ensures that returning early does not create a negative fine.

Why Strategy? Later, you could introduce a different fine calculator for different membership categories or book types without changing the return workflow.

One production improvement would be to use BigDecimal rather than double for monetary values.
